In contrast, AndroidView is a way to use Jetpack Compose with existing views in your layout files. It allows you to create custom views that you can use in your Compose code. The two approaches are very similar, but there is one key difference: ViewBinding uses code generation to generate code for each view in your layout, while AndroidView uses code generation to generate code for each custom view you create.

So when should you use ViewBinding instead of AndroidView? In general, if you’re using Compose and have a layout file that contains a lot of views, you might want to consider using ViewBinding. It can make it easier to reference views in your layout, and it can also make it easier to maintain your code as your layout changes. However, if you’re only using a few views in your layout, or if you’re not using Compose, then AndroidView might be a better choice.

