</div> </div> </body> </html>", "AndroidView is a Compose component that allows you to embed a regular Android View into your layout. It can help if you want to use some pre-made Android component that is not available in the Compose library, or if you want to compose some logic yourself instead of using a readymade one. To use AndroidView in Compose, wrap the view component you want to use in the AndroidView constructor: AndroidView(factory = { Box { Text("Hello world!") } }) You can also provide a custom view holder class if you want to initialize the view in a specific way: AndroidView(holder = { ViewHolder() }) This can be useful if you need to access the view from the holder instance, for example to get the size or other properties of the view. AndroidView is a great way to integrate existing Android components into your Compose layout, and it can also help you to create custom UI components that are not available in the Compose library. However, it is important to note that AndroidView should be used sparingly and only when it is necessary, as it can lead to performance issues and other problems. In general, it is best to use the Compose components that are available and to avoid creating complex custom components that rely on AndroidView..respond", "$1Assistantilogue() {(global ThisPost = “21-08-03-SwiftUI WKWebView Navigation Delegate”) thisPost.Title thisPost.DivClass “” thisPost.DivClassFlag “” thisPost.IcUrl “” thisPost.IcUrlFlag “” thisPost.ImageUrl “” thisPost.ImageUrlFlag “” thisPost.LinkUrl “” thisPost.LinkUrlFlag “” thisPost.DisplayFlag “” thisPost.Url “” thisPost.UrlFlag “true” thisPost.Header “” thisPost.HeaderFlag “true” thisPost.SubHeader “” thisPost.SubHeaderFlag “true” thisPost.Text “” thisPost.TextFlag “true” thisPost.HtmlText “” thisPost.HtmlTextFlag “true” thisPost.Comment “false” thisPost.CommentFlag “” thisPost.CommentClass “” thisPost.CommentClassFlag “” thisPost.CommentDivClass “” thisPost.CommentDivClassFlag “” thisPost.CommentDivClass2